"1\n00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:04,175\nThe Learning Brain. Series 7: Digital Learning\n\n2\n00:00:06,100 --> 00:00:07,336\nEpisode 6\n\n3\n00:00:07,416 --> 00:00:09,252\nSpeed-watching\n\n4\n00:00:12,052 --> 00:00:16,168\nMany students watch lecture videos at one and a half or double speed.\n\n5\n00:00:16,503 --> 00:00:18,153\nMany instructors wince.\n\n6\n00:00:18,412 --> 00:00:20,156\nAre students learning less?\n\n7\n00:00:20,326 --> 00:00:23,423\nIn a 2022 study at UCLA,\n\n8\n00:00:23,661 --> 00:00:27,118\nDillon Murphy, Alan Castel and colleagues tested it.\n\n9\n00:00:27,663 --> 00:00:30,031\nStudents watched short lecture videos,\n\n10\n00:00:30,293 --> 00:00:34,091\none on the Roman Empire and one on real estate appraisal,\n\n11\n00:00:34,292 --> 00:00:37,030\nat normal speed, one and a half times,\n\n12\n00:00:37,050 --> 00:00:39,279\ndouble, or two and a half times speed.\n\n13\n00:00:39,925 --> 00:00:44,988\nThey found that about eighty five percent of the students they surveyed already speed watch.\n\n14\n00:00:45,158 --> 00:00:47,200\nOn an immediate forty question test,\n\n15\n00:00:47,429 --> 00:00:50,716\nthe normal speed group averaged about twenty six correct,\n\n16\n00:00:50,974 --> 00:00:53,333\nand the double speed group about twenty five.\n\n17\n00:00:53,915 --> 00:00:55,770\nAt two and a half times speed,\n\n18\n00:00:55,831 --> 00:00:57,936\nscores fell to about twenty two.\n\n19\n00:00:58,484 --> 00:01:01,760\nOverall, costs were minimal up to double speed,\n\n20\n00:01:01,974 --> 00:01:04,127\nand performance declined beyond it.\n\n21\n00:01:04,297 --> 00:01:05,364\nThere was a twist.\n\n22\n00:01:05,798 --> 00:01:13,795\nStudents who watched again at double speed shortly before the test did better than those who watched once at normal speed a week earlier.\n\n23\n00:01:13,965 --> 00:01:14,881\nSome caution.\n\n24\n00:01:15,336 --> 00:01:17,802\nThese were short videos and lab tests.\n\n25\n00:01:18,150 --> 00:01:22,661\nThe authors warn that faster playback may not suit complex material,\n\n26\n00:01:22,895 --> 00:01:26,433\nand they did not recommend speeding up simply to save time.\n\n27\n00:01:26,603 --> 00:01:30,235\nThe real question is what students do with the time they save.\n\n28\n00:01:30,688 --> 00:01:32,166\nSpent on practice testing,\n\n29\n00:01:32,186 --> 00:01:32,871\nit helps.\n\n30\n00:01:33,201 --> 00:01:36,807\nSpent on nothing, it is just a faster way to watch.\n\n31\n00:01:36,977 --> 00:01:37,738\nFor teaching.\n\n32\n00:01:38,070 --> 00:01:39,815\nDon't ban speed controls.\n\n33\n00:01:40,019 --> 00:01:46,625\nInstead, tell students the evidence: up to double speed is usually fine,\n\n34\n00:01:46,645 --> 00:01:48,537\nslow down for hard parts,\n\n35\n00:01:48,952 --> 00:01:51,037\nand use saved time for practice.\n\n36\n00:01:51,057 --> 00:01:52,981\nKeep your own speech clear,\n\n37\n00:01:53,371 --> 00:01:55,240\nso faster playback stays understandable.\n\n38\n00:01:55,438 --> 00:01:56,380\nOne tip to share with students: if you speed up,\n\n39\n00:01:56,502 --> 00:01:58,691\nspend the saved time on practice.\n\n40\n00:01:58,861 --> 00:02:00,107\nFaster is fine.\n\n41\n00:02:00,303 --> 00:02:02,252\nFaster with practice is better.\n\n"